==Production==
===Development===
The series was renewed for a third season in March 2013.<ref name="season3"/> [[Jonathan Nolan]] stated that he viewed the season as two halves: the first half focusing on Carter's journey and the second half focusing more on the Machine storyline.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.buzzfeed.com/kateaurthur/why-person-of-interest-is-the-most-subversive-show-on-televi|title=Why "Person Of Interest" Is The Most Subversive Show On Television|last=Aurthur|first=Kate|date=May 6, 2014|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[BuzzFeed]]}}</ref> Nolan commented on the 22-episode season order, "It's very difficult. The number of episodes is probably calibrated not to the length of the season but to the exact point a showrunner will have a nervous breakdown."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2014/01/tca-cbs-showrunners-talk-large-episode-orders-killing-off-characters-and-their-breakdown-point-664905/|title=TCA: CBS Showrunners Talk Large Episode Orders, Killing Off Characters, And Their Breakdown Point|date=January 15, 2014|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>

===Writing===
[[Image:Edward Snowden-2.jpg{{!}}border|thumb|right|200px|During the season, many critics and analysts noticed parallelisms between the series' premise and [[Edward Snowden]].]]

Greg Plageman said that the Machine's location is not the central theme of the season, but what its intentions are, "What is it up to? What sort of organism is it becoming? And is it expanding beyond the parameters of what even Finch designed?"<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/09/03/fall-tv-spoilers-2013-shows-ncis/|title=Fall TV Spoiler Spectacular: Exclusive Scoop and Photos on 45 Returning Favorites!|date=September 3, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

Amy Acker teased her role in the season, "The Machine and Root have a very special connection — at least in her mind. So it's either perpetuating her fantasies by calling her or something else." She also addressed more scenes with Shaw, saying, "She's so great, I would love to torture her again any day."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/06/27/revenge-teen-wolf-under-the-dome-true-blood-spoilers/|title=Matt's Inside Line: Scoop on Revenge, Teen Wolf, Under the Dome, POI, True Blood, Suits and More|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=June 27, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> Plageman added that the season would explore mostly Carter and Shaw's pasts.<ref name="season3preview">{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/09/24/person-of-interest-season-3-preview/|title=Person of Interest: What (and Who) Is New for Season 3? And What Is The Machine Turning Into?|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=September 24, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> On Shaw's role, Plageman deemed her part of the "[[Groucho Marx]] School", explaining that she would not work for anyone following her government's betrayal but she would help the team "when she feels like it."<ref name="season3preview"/> Sarah Shahi commented on her character's past on "[[Razgovor]]", "as we were shooting it, I felt like it was a complete and total disaster and I had no idea how it was going to turn out. But then after watching it I was very pleased with it."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/10/29/person-of-interest-season-3-preview-sarah-shahi-shaw-root/|title=Person of Interest: Sarah Shahi Ponders Shaw's Sex Life (Um, Knives?), Talks Love/Hate With Root|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=October 29, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

Many analysts noticed similarities between the season's airing and [[Edward Snowden]], whose actions drew parallelisms to the series' premise, particularly to an episode in the first season, "[[No Good Deed (Person of Interest)|No Good Deed]]".<ref name="New Yorker">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.newyorker.com/culture/culture-desk/person-of-interest-the-tv-show-that-predicted-edward-snowden|title="Person of Interest": The TV Show That Predicted Edward Snowden|last=Rothman|first=Joshua|date=January 14, 2014|access-date=September 20, 2021|magazine=[[The New Yorker]]}}</ref> Nolan commented, "Occasionally, we've been accused of writing a show that's sort of an apologia for the [[Mass surveillance#Surveillance state|surveillance state]]. I totally reject that. If there's a cynicism to the show, it's in the premise not just that this is inevitable but that it's already happened. The science-fiction part of the show is that the Machine is accurate, but the invasion of civil liberties is not imaginary. If the legacy of the [[World War II|Second World War]] was the atomic age, then what might emerge from the war on terror would be artificial intelligence."<ref name="New Yorker"/> Plageman said, "What's interesting about the revelations coming from Snowden is that there are other characters, other figures — some who work for the [[Federal Bureau of Investigation|FBI]] or the [[National Security Agency|NSA]] — who indicated years ago that this was something that was going on. Certainly [[Shane Harris]] outlined all this in his book ''[[The Watchers: The Rise of America's Surveillance State|The Watchers]]'', going back as far as [[John Poindexter|Admiral Poindexter]], so what we'd like to do this year is inform people how long this has actually been going on, through drama and through fiction, but in a way that is actually quite real."<ref name="season3preview"/> Actor [[Kevin Chapman]] said, "Look at the NSA — they're making us look like a reality show! We've been talking about that for three seasons, and it's now just coming to light."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/person-interest-cast-creator-jonathan-643386/|title='Person of Interest' Actor Says the NSA Is 'Making Us Look Like a Reality Show'|last=Lee|first=Ashley|date=October 4, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref>

===Casting===
[[Image:Taraji P. Henson 2011.jpg|thumb|right|200px|This was the last season with [[Taraji P. Henson]] as series regular due to her character's death. She would return for a guest performance in the fourth season.]]

A few days after the second season ended, it was announced that [[Sarah Shahi]], who played Sameen Shaw, would be upgraded to series regular starting with this season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/person-interest-adds-sarah-shahi-523213/|title='Person of Interest' Adds Sarah Shahi as Series Regular|last=Ng|first=Phyliana|date=May 15, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> In July 2013, it was reported that [[Amy Acker]] was also upgraded to series regular for the season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/comic-con-amy-acker-upped-589557/|title=Comic-Con: Amy Acker Upped to Series Regular on 'Person of Interest'|last=O'Connell|first=Mikey|date=July 20, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> Nolan commented on the decision to upgrade both stars, "when you add a few new characters, that actually winds up adding five or six new relationships. It gives us that much more of a challenge, which is great fun."<ref name="PaleyFest">{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2013/10/person-of-interest-at-ny-paleyfest-spotlight-on-new-regulars-sarah-shahi-amy-acker-603037/|title='Person Of Interest' At NY PaleyFest: Spotlight On New Regulars Sarah Shahi & Amy Acker|date=October 3, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>

The season brought back many actors from the series, including [[Paige Turco]] as Zoe Morgan, who reprised her role on "[[Lady Killer (Person of Interest)|Lady Killer]]".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/POIWritersRoom/status/365191164191731712|title=#ladiesnight|work=[[Twitter]]|date=August 7, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/08/08/person-of-interest-season-3-paige-turco-returns-zoe/|title=Sneak Peek: Person of Interest Unleashes Its 'Secret Weapons' Zoe, Carter and Shaw|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=August 8, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

"[[The Crossing (Person of Interest)|The Crossing]]" features the death of Joss Carter, played by [[Taraji P. Henson]]. During rumors of a main character death, recurring cast member [[Carrie Preston]] teased the death, "It's going to be sad for everybody — not just the fans but also the cast. But I think the way [the show] is handling it is quite remarkable."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/11/19/greys-anatomy-april-matthew-wedding-season-10-photo-spoilers/|title=Ask Ausiello: Spoilers on Grey's, Mentalist, NCIS, Nikita, OITNB, Banshee, Maids, POI and More|last=Ausiello|first=Michael|date=November 19, 2013|access-date=June 26, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/10/17/blind-item-ausiello-series-kill-off-star/|title=Blind Item: Hit Series Poised to Kill Off Star|last=Ausiello|first=Michael|date=October 17, 2013|access-date=June 26, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> Nolan told Henson about her death at the beginning of 2013, indicating, "It was a bittersweet conversation, because we love working with her and vice versa. It's been a great creative collaboration. As sad as we were to see her go, I think we were all excited [Taraji, us and the writers] to get into a juicy piece of material, to tell a tragedy." He went on to explain the decision, "For this story [HR, police corruption] it was a natural boiling point that would put her first and foremost into focus. And frankly, as writers, we've long said that Carter was the heart of the show, and your perverse impulse as a writer is to do as much damage to the audience as possible. There's nothing more dastardly than — if Carter's the heart of the show — breaking that heart for the audience in the middle of the season."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/person-interest-eps-carters-death-658306/|title='Person of Interest' Bosses Talk Major Death, Team Fallout and Reese Unhinged (Q&A)|last=Ng|first=Philiana|date=November 20, 2013|access-date=June 26, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> Executive producer Greg Plageman also commented on the decision, "What felt natural to us was with HR being with us from the pilot, their diabolical no-goodness, it felt like they had it in for Carter... it got to a point where you think if this person is going to hold her head up and walk into a precinct ever again she's going to have to take them on and in the conversations she had with her son, it was kind of understood what she had to do and it felt like the time was right."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.tvfanatic.com/2013/11/person-of-interest-post-mortem-bosses-explain-shocking-kiss-char/|title=Person of Interest Post-Mortem: Bosses Explain Shocking Kiss, Character Death|last=Halterman|first=Jim|date=November 20, 2013|access-date=June 26, 2021|work=TV Fanatic}}</ref> The kiss scene between Reese and Carter was unscripted, with Nolan commenting, "It was just a swapping scars moment. So when the actors went there, it was all of their own volition, because in that moment they both felt it. And when you removed that element, the scene didn't feel quite the same."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/11/20/person-of-interest-season-3-why-carter-was-killed-off/|title=Person of Interest EPs Defend Shocking Twist, Talk Unscripted Kiss, Tease 'Messy' Aftermath|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=November 20, 2013|access-date=June 26, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

Among the many guest stars in the season include [[Warren Kole]] as Ian Murphy, "a POI whom Detective Carter, Agent Shaw and Zoe Morgan each try to catch the eye of — by any dressed-to-kill means necessary."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/08/23/person-of-interest-season-3-warren-kole-ladykiller/|title=Person of Interest Scoop: The Following's Warren Kole Cast as a Real Ladykiller|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=August 23, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> For the first episode, [[Rey Valentin]] was cast as Jack Salazar, "a sailor on shore leave — during [[Fleet Week]] in New York City."<ref name="season3preview"/> The fourth episode guest starred [[Kathleen Rose Perkins]] as Vanessa Watkins, "a former ADA who is captured by the police for murdering her defense attorney ex-husband."<ref name="season3preview"/> In November 2013, [[Saul Rubinek]] joined for a two-episode guest performance as Arthur Claypool, a character only described as "slowly losing his mind."<ref name="Claypool"/>

In July 2013, [[Leslie Odom Jr.]] joined the series in a recurring role as Peter Collier, "an educated, even-tempered tech executive. Highly analytical, he plays his cards close to the vest."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2013/07/michelle-forbes-to-recur-on-chicago-fire-leslie-odom-jr-on-person-of-interest-547357/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140831032556/http://deadline.com/2013/07/michelle-forbes-to-recur-on-chicago-fire-leslie-odom-jr-on-person-of-interest-547357/|url-status=dead|archive-date=August 31, 2014|title=Michelle Forbes To Recur On 'Chicago Fire', Leslie Odom, Jr On 'Person Of Interest'|last=Andreeva|first=Nellie|date=July 23, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> In November 2013, [[Camryn Manheim]] joined the series as Diane, Arthur Claypool's wife.<ref name="Claypool">{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/11/12/person-of-interest-season-3-cast-camryn-manheim-saul-rubinek/|title=Exclusive: Person of Interest Recruits Practice Alum, Warehouse 13 Vet for Big Arc|last=Ausiello|first=Michael|date=November 12, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> She debuted on "[[Lethe (Person of Interest)|Lethe]]", where her character was revealed to be Control.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2014/01/06/person-of-interest-season-3-winter-preview-second-machine/|title=Person of Interest EPs Tease Search for Second Machine, 'Cluster#@¢% of Villains' to Come|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=January 6, 2014|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> The character was teased on "[[God Mode (Person of Interest)|God Mode]]", where she was played unseen by an uncredited actress.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/03/28/scandal-greys-anatomy-person-interest-spoilers/|title=Matt's Inside Line: Scoop on Scandal, Red Widow, Once, Grey's, POI, Beauty and the Beast and More|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=March 28, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

==Release==
===Broadcast===
In May 2013, CBS announced that the series would move from its Thursday time slot to Tuesdays at 10 p.m.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/05/15/cbs-fall-tv-lineup-2013/|title=CBS Fall Schedule Revealed: Thursday Comedy Block Expands, Person of Interest, Hawaii Five-0 on the Move, Mike & Molly Held to Midseason|last=Ausiello|first=Michael|date=May 15, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> Many deemed the move favorable for the series, as CBS wanted to create a second comedy block on Thursday night and considered that pairing the series with ''[[NCIS (TV series)|NCIS]]'' and ''[[NCIS: Los Angeles]]'' would work on its favor.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/05/21/fall-tv-schedule-2013-review-bones-shield-once-upon-a-time/|title=Fall TV Schedule Moves in Review: The Savvy, the Shocking and the Sorta Snoozeworthy|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=May 21, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> In June 2013, CBS announced that the season would premiere on September 24, 2013.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/06/17/cbs-premiere-dates-2013-fall/|title=Fall TV: CBS Launch Dates Include How I Met Your Mother's Hour-Long Final Premiere|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=June 17, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> The season ended on May 13, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2014/03/20/cbs-season-finale-dates-2014-ncis-big-bang-theory-good-wife/|title=CBS Sets Season Finale Dates for NCIS, Big Bang Theory, The Good Wife, Survivor and Others|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=March 20, 2014|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

===Marketing===
On July 20, 2013, the cast and crew attended the 2013 [[San Diego Comic-Con]] to discuss and promote the season and show a sneak peek. The panel, which revealed that [[Amy Acker]] was upgraded to series regular, also included a short reel which included Finch commenting on the [[PRISM (surveillance program)|PRISM]] disclosure.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://collider.com/comic-con-person-of-interest-panel-recap/|title=Comic-Con: PERSON OF INTEREST Panel Recap; Cast and Crew Discuss the NSA Prism Effect and Announce Amy Acker as Series Regular|last=Howard|first=Rachel|date=July 21, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]}}</ref> In October 2013, the cast attended the 2013 [[Paley Center for Media|Paley Fest]] and [[New York Comic Con]] for a Q&A session and show a sneak peek of an upcoming episode.<ref name="PaleyFest"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvweb.com/person-of-interest-season-3-new-york-comic-con-2013-sneak-peek/|title=Person of Interest Season 3 New York Comic Con 2013 Sneak Peek|last=Orange|first=B. Alan|date=October 14, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=TVWeb.com}}</ref>

As part of a stunt for the [[Nielsen ratings#Sweeps|November sweeps]], CBS promoted "[[Endgame (Person of Interest)|Endgame]]", "[[The Crossing (Person of Interest)|The Crossing]]" and "[[The Devil's Share]]" as part of a "three-episode event", which was deemed as pivotal and would change the direction of the series. Nolan and Plageman teased the episodes, "We promised our actors and our audience that these characters wouldn't be static, stuck in an endless loop — that they would have a journey. And, of course, every journey comes to an end."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://tvline.com/2013/11/05/person-of-interest-season-3-november-sweeps-promo-fusco/|title=Exclusive: As Person of Interest Touts Dark Twist, EPs Warn: 'Every Journey Comes to an End'|last=Webb Mitovich|first=Matt|date=November 5, 2013|access-date=September 20, 2021|work=[[TVLine]]|archive-date=September 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210921034133/https://tvline.com/2013/11/05/person-of-interest-season-3-november-sweeps-promo-fusco/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Home media release===
The third season was released on Blu-ray and DVD in [[DVD region code#Region codes and countries|region 1]] on September 2, 2014,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tvshowsondvd.com/news/Person-Season-3/19910|title=Person of Interest – Can We Interest You In an Announcement for 'The Complete 3rd Season'?|publisher=TVShowsOnDVD.com|last=Lambert|first=David|date=June 10, 2014|access-date=June 11, 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140613094504/http://www.tvshowsondvd.com/news/Person-Season-3/19910|archive-date=June 13, 2014|df=mdy-all}}</ref> in region 2 on September 21, 2015,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.co.uk/dp/B00S80X092/|title=Person of Interest - Season 3 [Blu-ray] [Region Free]|publisher=Amazon.co.uk|access-date=March 30, 2015}}</ref> and in region 4 on October 8, 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ezydvd.com.au/blu-ray/person-of-interest-season-3/dp/6156236|title=Person of Interest: Season 3|publisher=EzyDVD|access-date=November 12, 2014}}</ref>

In 2014, [[Warner Bros. Television Studios]] announced that it sold the off-network [[Video on demand#Subscription models|SVOD]] of the series to [[Netflix]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2014/10/person-of-interest-netflix-jj-abrams-license-deal-warner-bros-cbs-852003/|title='Person of Interest' Licensed To Netflix In Warner Bros Deal|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=October 15, 2014|access-date=September 26, 2021}}</ref> On September 1, 2015, the season became available to stream on Netflix.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/netflix-new-releases-september-2015-movies-tv-818943/|title=Netflix New Releases Coming in September 2015|website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=August 31, 2015|access-date=September 26, 2021}}</ref> On September 22, 2020, the series left the service and was added to [[HBO Max]] on January 23, 2021.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://popculture.com/streaming/news/person-of-interest-leaving-netflix-september/|title='Person of Interest' Leaving Netflix in September|website=PopCulture.com|last=Schonter|first=Allison|date=August 25, 2020|access-date=September 26, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/erikkain/2021/01/06/hbo-max-new-tv-shows-movies-january-2021-wonder-woman-leaving/|title=Everything Coming To HBO Max In January 2021 — And Everything Leaving|website=[[Forbes]]|last=Kain|first=Erik|date=January 6, 2021|access-date=September 26, 2021}}</ref>

